  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nCurrent analysis reveals that the cold front we have been watching \nremains just to our NW, extending from Lake Superior down through \ncentral KS. The 500mb trough currently over the Rocky Mountains will \ngradually move east into the Central Plains, pushing the cold front \nthrough our area this evening and through the overnight hours. WAA \nadvection ahead of the cold front, however, will keep highs this \nafternoon in the upper 60s and low 70s. The pressure gradient ahead \nof the front will remain fairly tight keeping breezy and gusty \nconditions around through this evening with sustained southerly \nwinds around 15-20 mph and gusts up to 30 mph. Showers will begin to \nmove into NW MO this evening and will gradually overspread the \nforecast area by midnight. There is some instability along the front \nso there is up to a 25% chance for isolated thunderstorms as the \nfront moves through. However, severe risk remains low with MUCAPE \nvalues generally around 100 J/kg or lower and more stable lapse \nrates, despite shear values around 40-50kts. Showers should move out \nof the area by mid morning Sunday. Strong CAA and northerly surface \nflow will bring back colder temperatures with highs in the upper 40s \nto low 50s for Sunday afternoon. The pressure gradient will remain \nfairly tight behind the cold front with high pressure building in \nfrom the Central Plains. Therefore, breezy and gusty conditions will \ncontinue Sunday with winds out of the NNW around 10-15 mph and gusts \nup to 25 mph. Surface high pressure will continue to build into the \nforecast area Sunday night and the pressure gradient will weaken as \nthe cold front continues to move east. Therefore, winds will be \nlight and skies will clear, allowing for radiational cooling and \novernight lows that dip into the mid to upper 20s.\n\nMonday and Tuesday's forecast will be dominated by the broad area of \nsurface high pressure over the Midwest and quasi-zonal 500mb flow. \nThis will keep things dry and cool with highs in the 40s and \novernight lows in the low 20s. Tuesday night into Wednesday, a 500mb \ntrough will dig through the Midwest with a closed low over AR, which \nwill keep rain chances to our south. Another 500mb low digs into MO \nThursday but NNW flow and high pressure at the surface will keep \nrain chances east of our area and highs will remain in the mid to \nupper 40s. We could see a slight warmup Friday and Saturday as the \n500mb trough and surface high move just east of the forecast area \nand WAA advection supports highs in the upper 40s and low 50s.",
